## Why This Is Asked

This question tests adaptive sorting algorithms, merge logic, and the ability to reason about time complexity in practical, maintainable ways.

## Key Concepts

- **Adaptive sorting**: Using a pre-check to achieve O(n) best-case performance when the array is already sorted
- **Iterative bottom-up mergesort**: Avoiding recursion by iteratively merging sorted subarrays of increasing size
- **Stability and memory usage**: Understanding the trade-offs of O(n) auxiliary space for guaranteed stability

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortWithEarlyMerge(arr) {
  // Early return if already sorted
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) break;
    if (i === arr.length - 1) return arr;
  }

  // Iterative bottom-up mergesort
  const aux = [...arr];
  let width = 1;

  while (width < arr.length) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += width * 2) {
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, arr.length);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, arr.length);

      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right) {
        aux[k++] = arr[p] <= arr[q] ? arr[p++] : arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
    }

    [arr, aux] = [aux, arr];
    width *= 2;
  }

  return arr;
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis

- **Best case (O(n))**: Array is already sorted; single linear scan confirms this
- **Worst case (O(n log n))**: Array is reverse sorted; full mergesort required
- **Space complexity**: O(n) for the auxiliary buffer

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine shortest-path computation, path counting, and path reconstruction into a cohesive solution. It also evaluates handling of multiple equal-cost routes and lexicographic tie-breaking.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm for O(m log n) shortest paths
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological ordering by distance values
- DP for counting distinct shortest paths
- Lexicographic path reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestStats(n, edges, s, t) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
  }

  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const pq = [[0, s]];
  dist[s] = 0;

  while (pq.length) {
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        pq.push([dist[v], v]);
      }
    }
  }

  const dag = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (let u = 0; u < n; u++) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        dag[u].push(v);
      }
    }
  }

  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  const order = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i)
    .sort((a, b) => dist[a] - dist[b]);

  for (const u of order) {
    for (const v of dag[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }

  const path = [];
  let curr = t;
  while (curr !== null) {
    path.unshift(curr);
    const preds = dag
      .map((list, u) => list.includes(curr) ? u : -1)
      .filter(u => u >= 0 && dist[curr] === dist[u] + 
        adj[u].find(([v]) => v === curr)?.[1]);
    preds.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    curr = preds.length > 0 ? preds[0] : null;
    if (curr === s) {
      path.unshift(s);
      break;
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t], ways: ways[t], path };
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Dijkstra: O(m log n)
- DAG construction: O(m)
- DP counting: O(m)
- Path reconstruction: O(n + m)
- Overall: O(m log n)

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses understanding of stability in sorting and practical merge logic, not just raw sorting.

## Key Concepts

